course: dictionary
lesson: 11193
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Wit has more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one you need. Read the words around it, because they are its context. «She finds a little house, and, so as not to frighten them out of their wits, she nibbles the right-hand bit.» Here wits means your ability to think clearly, so the sentence is about being scared, not about being funny.
explain: Wit can also mean saying clever and funny things quickly. Someone who does this is a wit. Look for jokes, puns or quick answers in a text. When a character gives one, the writer is showing wit, and that is a clue to the meaning.
explain: Some phrases with wit are fixed, so learn them as a set. «Keep your wits about you» means stay alert. «At your wits' end» means so worried you do not know what to do. «Live on your wits» means getting by using your own cleverness.
fact: Wit means the ability to say clever and funny things quickly.
fact: A wit is a person who is clever and funny with words.
fact: Wits (plural) means your ability to think clearly and quickly.
fact: «Keep your wits about you» means stay alert and ready.
fact: «To wit» is an old, formal way of saying «that is to say» or «namely».
exercise: In «frighten them out of their wits», what does wits mean? | Their shoes | Their clothes | Their jokes | *Their ability to think clearly
exercise: «Oscar Wilde was a famous wit.» What does wit mean here? | *A person who is clever and funny with words | A long journey | A kind of hat | A small house
exercise: Which phrase means stay alert? | Battle of words | Wits' end | *Keep your wits about you | Scared to death
test: Which phrase means so worried you do not know what to do? | *At your wits' end | Battle of wits | Live on your wits | Quick wit
test: What does «to wit» mean? | Sleepy and slow | *That is to say, or namely | A question you must answer | Very funny
exam: A character makes a quick, funny reply in a story, and the writer is showing this skill. What is it called? | Dullness | *Wit | Hunger | Fear
